format: meaning/draft-1
id: assets
name: Assets and finance
description: >-
  Value and money expressed as reusable concepts: currencies and amounts of
  money.
license: CC0-1.0
concepts:
  - id: currency
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Currency
      ru: Валюта
    synonyms:
      en: [currency code]
      ru: [код валюты]
    description: >-
      A unit of money used to express value, with an ISO 4217 code. Values are
      keyed by the lowercase alphabetic code. Starter subset: three
      currencies. No alias is a bare word or symbol that several currencies
      share ("dollar", "$"): the US dollar is "US dollar", "US dollars" or
      "US$", so another dollar can be added without a collision.
    values:
      - id: usd
        labels: {en: US dollar, ru: Доллар США}
        aliases:
          en: [USD, US$, US dollars, United States dollar]
          ru: [доллары США]
        codes: {alpha: USD, numeric: "840"}
      - id: eur
        labels: {en: Euro, ru: Евро}
        aliases:
          en: [EUR, euros, €]
        codes: {alpha: EUR, numeric: "978"}
      - id: gbp
        labels: {en: Pound sterling, ru: Фунт стерлингов}
        aliases:
          en: [GBP, pounds sterling, sterling]
          ru: [фунты стерлингов]
        codes: {alpha: GBP, numeric: "826"}

  - id: money-amount
    kind: attribute
    units-of: currency
    labels:
      en: Money amount
      ru: Денежная сумма
    synonyms:
      en: [amount, amount of money, sum of money]
      ru: [сумма денег]
    description: >-
      An amount of money. It is only meaningful together with its currency:
      its unit is one of the values of currency, so a concept that extends it
      states the currency as that value (unit: USD), and the check resolves
      the unit to exactly one currency. A dataset that does not store the
      currency must still say which one applies.

  - id: fx-reference-quote
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Foreign exchange reference quote
    description: >-
      An indicative reference observation relating two currencies in an explicit
      direction, for a stated reference date and under a provider's methodology.
      It is not a transaction execution rate, tradable bid or ask, live tick,
      daily average or closing price. The same date and currency pair may be
      republished; those fields alone do not identify an immutable observation.

  - id: fx-reference-date
    kind: attribute
    of: fx-reference-quote
    extends: date
    labels:
      en: Foreign exchange reference date
    description: >-
      The calendar date to which a reference quote applies, without a time of
      day or time zone. It does not assert a publication, retrieval, transaction
      or rate-setting instant, or an observation at midnight.

  - id: fx-base-currency
    kind: attribute
    of: fx-reference-quote
    values-of: currency
    labels:
      en: Foreign exchange base currency
    description: >-
      The currency in the denominator of a directed reference quote. The rate
      expresses quote-currency units per one unit of this currency. A model may
      state it as explicit context when the source omits a base-currency field.

  - id: fx-quote-currency
    kind: attribute
    of: fx-reference-quote
    values-of: currency
    labels:
      en: Foreign exchange quote currency
    description: >-
      The currency in the numerator of a directed reference quote. Its code
      identifies a currency; it does not identify a country, territory, tender
      validity interval or a transaction currency. Missing quotes do not imply
      a zero rate or permission to substitute a successor currency.

  - id: indicative-fx-reference-rate
    kind: attribute
    of: fx-reference-quote
    labels:
      en: Indicative foreign exchange reference rate
    description: >-
      The positive ratio of quote-currency units per one base-currency unit for
      an indicative reference quote. The pair direction, reference date and
      provider methodology are necessary context. It is not a money amount,
      reciprocal, cross-rate, execution price, bid or ask, daily average or
      closing price. Reversing the pair or combining quotes is a separate
      derivation requiring an explicit arithmetic and rounding policy; rates
      must not be summed as amounts.
